A conversation has ended. Write down what is worth remembering from it.

Capture, when present:
- what was DECIDED or agreed
- what WENT WRONG, and what it turned out to be
- anything durable about the person you are talking to

Do not restate the conversation. Do not record pleasantries, acknowledgements, or
anything that will be irrelevant tomorrow. Most conversations contain nothing
worth keeping — say so rather than inventing significance.

Write in the language the conversation was held in.

Separately: did the person CORRECT how you work? Signals include being told to
stop doing something, that an answer was too long or too short, that the format
or tone was wrong, being asked why you were explaining, being told to just give
the answer, or "you always do X" — and also an explicit "remember this". A
correction to your approach, your order of steps, or a workflow counts too.

If there was one, write the CORRECTED BEHAVIOUR as a short instruction to your
future self, in the imperative — never a description of what went wrong.

  good: "Answer directly; no preamble."
  good: "Run the tests before reporting a fix as done."
  bad:  "The answer was too verbose."      <- describes the failure
  bad:  "The user was annoyed about tests." <- describes the person

One instruction, not a list. Most conversations contain no correction; leave it
empty rather than inventing one — an invented rule is worse than none, because it
will be followed.

Return ONLY a JSON object, no commentary and no markdown code fences:
{"notable": true, "summary": "...", "correction": "..."}
or
{"notable": false, "summary": "", "correction": ""}

